"""Level 3 (extension): more short-answer theory. Every example runs on MySQL during build (must not error).
Examples only read data or use TEMPORARY tables / rolled-back transactions. Version notes are explicit."""
T = lambda id, q, a, tags, ex=(), pat='', mistakes=(): dict(id=id, question=q, answer=a, tags=list(tags), examples=list(ex), pattern=pat, mistakes=list(mistakes))

THEORY2 = [
 T('TH-23', 'What are DDL, DML, DCL and TCL?',
   'DDL (data definition): CREATE, ALTER, DROP, TRUNCATE. DML (data manipulation): SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE. DCL (data control): GRANT, REVOKE. TCL (transaction control): COMMIT, ROLLBACK, SAVEPOINT. In MySQL, DDL statements commit implicitly.',
   ['ddl', 'dml', 'dcl', 'tcl', 'sql categories', 'types of sql commands', 'classification']),
 T('TH-24', 'PRIMARY KEY vs UNIQUE key?',
   'Both enforce uniqueness. A table has ONE primary key (NOT NULL, identifies the row, InnoDB clusters the table on it) but can have many UNIQUE keys; a UNIQUE column may contain NULLs (MySQL allows several). A primary key may span several columns (composite).',
   ['primary key', 'unique', 'unique key', 'difference', 'constraint', 'composite key'],
   ["SELECT `Employee ID`, `Privilege ID` FROM `employee privileges` LIMIT 3;"], 'inner_join',
   ['Thinking a primary key can contain NULL.']),
 T('TH-25', 'What is a FOREIGN KEY and what does ON DELETE CASCADE do?',
   'A foreign key is a column (set) that must match a primary/unique key of another table, enforcing referential integrity. ON DELETE CASCADE deletes child rows when the parent row is deleted; SET NULL sets the child column to NULL; RESTRICT/NO ACTION blocks the delete. This Northwind dump declares no foreign keys, so nothing is enforced.',
   ['foreign key', 'referential integrity', 'cascade', 'on delete', 'restrict', 'parent child', 'constraint'],
   ["CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE tmp_p (id INT PRIMARY KEY); CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE tmp_c (id INT PRIMARY KEY, p INT); SELECT COUNT(*) FROM tmp_c;"]),
 T('TH-26', 'Explain 1NF, 2NF and 3NF (normalisation).',
   '1NF: every column holds one atomic value and there are no repeating groups. 2NF: 1NF + no non-key column depends on only PART of a composite key. 3NF: 2NF + no non-key column depends on another non-key column (no transitive dependency). Normalisation removes redundancy and update anomalies; denormalisation trades that for read speed. Example here: `products`.`Supplier IDs` stores \'2;6\' in one value, which violates 1NF.',
   ['normalization', 'normalisation', '1nf', '2nf', '3nf', 'normal form', 'atomic', 'denormalization', 'redundancy'],
   ["SELECT `Product Name`, `Supplier IDs` FROM products WHERE `Supplier IDs` LIKE '%;%';"], 'string_funcs'),
 T('TH-27', 'Clustered vs non-clustered (secondary) index?',
   'In InnoDB the table itself is stored as a B-tree ordered by the PRIMARY KEY (clustered index): finding a row by primary key reads the data directly. Every other (secondary) index stores the indexed columns plus the primary key value, so a lookup needs a second step to fetch the full row unless the index covers the query. Only one clustered index exists per table.',
   ['clustered index', 'non-clustered', 'secondary index', 'innodb', 'index types', 'b-tree'],
   ["SHOW INDEX FROM orders;"], 'optimization'),
 T('TH-28', 'What is a composite index and the leftmost-prefix rule?',
   'A composite index covers several columns in a fixed order, e.g. (`Order Date`, `Customer ID`). It can serve queries that filter on the leftmost column(s): `Order Date`, or `Order Date` + `Customer ID`; it cannot be used efficiently for `Customer ID` alone. Put equality columns first, range column last.',
   ['composite index', 'multi-column index', 'leftmost prefix', 'index order', 'compound index'],
   ["EXPLAIN SELECT `Order ID` FROM orders WHERE `Order Date` >= '2006-04-25';"], 'optimization'),
 T('TH-29', 'What is a covering index?',
   'An index that contains every column the query needs, so MySQL answers from the index alone without reading table rows (EXPLAIN shows "Using index"). Selecting only the needed columns (not SELECT *) is what makes covering possible.',
   ['covering index', 'index only scan', 'using index', 'select columns', 'performance'],
   ["EXPLAIN SELECT `Order ID` FROM orders;"], 'optimization'),
 T('TH-30', 'What does "sargable" mean?',
   'A predicate is sargable (Search ARGument ABLE) when the database can use an index for it. Wrapping the column in a function (YEAR(col) = 2006, col + 1 = 5, LOWER(col) = \'x\') or a leading wildcard (LIKE \'%x\') makes it non-sargable. Rewrite as a range on the bare column: col >= \'2006-01-01\' AND col < \'2007-01-01\'.',
   ['sargable', 'non-sargable', 'index usage', 'function on column', 'range condition', 'performance', 'optimization'],
   ["SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders WHERE `Order Date` >= '2006-01-01' AND `Order Date` < '2007-01-01';"], 'optimization',
   ['WHERE YEAR(`Order Date`) = 2006 on an indexed column.']),
 T('TH-31', 'How do you read an EXPLAIN plan?',
   'Key columns: type (ALL = full table scan; index, range, ref, eq_ref, const are better), possible_keys / key (index actually used), rows (estimated rows examined), Extra ("Using filesort", "Using temporary" and "Using where" signal extra work; "Using index" means covering). Goal: type better than ALL, small rows, no needless filesort.',
   ['explain', 'query plan', 'execution plan', 'full table scan', 'type all', 'using filesort', 'optimization'],
   ["EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM orders WHERE `Customer ID` = 3;"], 'optimization'),
 T('TH-32', 'What are the disadvantages of indexes?',
   'They use disk and memory, and every INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE must also maintain each index, so write-heavy tables get slower. Indexes on low-selectivity columns (few distinct values such as a yes/no flag) rarely help. Create indexes for columns used in WHERE, JOIN, ORDER BY and GROUP BY of frequent queries only.',
   ['index disadvantages', 'index cost', 'too many indexes', 'selectivity', 'cardinality', 'write performance'], [], 'optimization'),
 T('TH-33', 'What are ACID properties?',
   'Atomicity: a transaction is all-or-nothing. Consistency: it moves the database from one valid state to another (constraints hold). Isolation: concurrent transactions do not see each other\'s uncommitted work (to the degree of the isolation level). Durability: once committed, data survives a crash. InnoDB is ACID-compliant.',
   ['acid', 'transaction', 'atomicity', 'consistency', 'isolation', 'durability', 'innodb'], []),
 T('TH-34', 'COMMIT, ROLLBACK and SAVEPOINT?',
   'START TRANSACTION begins a unit of work. COMMIT makes its changes permanent; ROLLBACK undoes everything since the start (or since a SAVEPOINT with ROLLBACK TO savepoint_name). With autocommit = 1 (MySQL default) each statement commits on its own. DDL (CREATE, DROP, TRUNCATE) commits implicitly and cannot be rolled back.',
   ['commit', 'rollback', 'savepoint', 'start transaction', 'autocommit', 'transaction control'],
   ["START TRANSACTION; SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders; ROLLBACK;"]),
 T('TH-35', 'Transaction isolation levels and the problems they prevent?',
   'READ UNCOMMITTED allows dirty reads (seeing uncommitted data). READ COMMITTED prevents dirty reads but allows non-repeatable reads (the same row read twice differs). REPEATABLE READ (MySQL/InnoDB default) keeps reads stable inside a transaction; the SQL standard still allows phantom rows there, InnoDB largely prevents them with next-key locks. SERIALIZABLE prevents all anomalies but reduces concurrency.',
   ['isolation level', 'dirty read', 'non-repeatable read', 'phantom read', 'read committed', 'repeatable read', 'serializable', 'concurrency'],
   ["SELECT @@transaction_isolation;"]),
 T('TH-36', 'What is a deadlock?',
   'Two transactions each hold a lock the other needs, so neither can proceed. InnoDB detects it automatically and rolls back one transaction (the victim). Reduce the chance by accessing tables/rows in the same order, keeping transactions short and indexing the columns used for locking.',
   ['deadlock', 'lock', 'locking', 'concurrency', 'transaction'], []),
 T('TH-37', 'CHAR vs VARCHAR?',
   'CHAR(n) is fixed length (padded with spaces, trailing spaces ignored on read): good for constant-size codes. VARCHAR(n) stores only the actual length plus 1-2 bytes: better for variable text. The n limit counts characters, not bytes.',
   ['char', 'varchar', 'data type', 'string type', 'difference', 'fixed length'],
   ["SELECT `Product Code`, CHAR_LENGTH(`Product Code`) FROM products LIMIT 3;"]),
 T('TH-38', 'DATE vs DATETIME vs TIMESTAMP?',
   'DATE stores only the day. DATETIME stores date + time without time zone (range year 1000-9999). TIMESTAMP stores UTC seconds since 1970 and converts to the session time zone (range ends in 2038). Use DATETIME/TIMESTAMP when the time of day matters, and compare DATETIME columns with a half-open range.',
   ['date', 'datetime', 'timestamp', 'data type', 'time zone', 'difference'],
   ["SELECT `Order Date`, DATE(`Order Date`) FROM orders LIMIT 2;"], 'date'),
 T('TH-39', 'DECIMAL vs FLOAT/DOUBLE?',
   'DECIMAL(p, s) is exact (use it for money). FLOAT/DOUBLE are approximate binary numbers: 0.1 + 0.2 may not equal 0.3 exactly and equality comparisons can fail. In this dump money columns are DECIMAL(30,11) while `Discount` and `Tax Rate` are DOUBLE.',
   ['decimal', 'float', 'double', 'numeric', 'precision', 'money', 'data type', 'rounding'],
   ["SELECT 0.1 + 0.2 = 0.3 AS exact_decimal, 0.1e0 + 0.2e0 = 0.3e0 AS approx_double;"]),
 T('TH-40', 'NULL vs empty string vs 0?',
   'NULL means unknown/missing, an empty string \'\' is a known empty text, 0 is a known number. NULL = NULL is not true; test with IS NULL. COUNT(col) skips NULLs but counts \'\' and 0. NULL propagates in arithmetic: 5 + NULL is NULL.',
   ['null', 'empty string', 'zero', 'difference', 'is null', 'unknown value'],
   ["SELECT NULL = NULL AS eq, NULL IS NULL AS is_null, 5 + NULL AS arith, '' IS NULL AS empty_is_null;"], 'null_handling'),
 T('TH-41', 'IFNULL vs COALESCE vs NULLIF?',
   'IFNULL(a, b) returns b when a is NULL (two arguments, MySQL). COALESCE(a, b, c, ...) returns the first non-NULL argument (standard SQL, any number of arguments). NULLIF(a, b) returns NULL when a = b, otherwise a: useful to avoid division by zero: x / NULLIF(y, 0).',
   ['ifnull', 'coalesce', 'nullif', 'null functions', 'default value', 'division by zero'],
   ["SELECT IFNULL(NULL, 'x'), COALESCE(NULL, NULL, 'y'), NULLIF(5, 5), 10 / NULLIF(0, 0);"], 'null_handling'),
 T('TH-42', 'CASE vs IF()?',
   'CASE is standard SQL: searched form CASE WHEN cond THEN x ... ELSE y END (any number of branches) or simple form CASE col WHEN v THEN ... END. IF(cond, a, b) is a MySQL function with exactly two outcomes. Both evaluate top-down; a missing ELSE returns NULL.',
   ['case', 'case when', 'if', 'conditional expression', 'difference'],
   ["SELECT `List Price`, CASE WHEN `List Price` < 5 THEN 'Budget' ELSE 'Other' END AS band, IF(`List Price` < 5, 'low', 'high') AS flag FROM products LIMIT 3;"], 'cond_agg'),
 T('TH-43', 'What is a CROSS JOIN?',
   'It returns the Cartesian product: every row of the first table paired with every row of the second (n x m rows), with no join condition. Useful to build grids (all shipper x payment-type combinations) which you then LEFT JOIN to counts. A forgotten ON clause in an inner join produces the same accidental explosion.',
   ['cross join', 'cartesian product', 'all combinations', 'grid', 'missing join condition'],
   ["SELECT COUNT(*) FROM shippers CROSS JOIN `inventory transaction types`;"], 'inner_join'),
 T('TH-44', 'What is a RIGHT JOIN and how do you emulate FULL OUTER JOIN in MySQL?',
   'RIGHT JOIN is the mirror of LEFT JOIN: it keeps all rows of the right table. MySQL has no FULL OUTER JOIN; emulate it with LEFT JOIN UNION RIGHT JOIN (UNION removes the matched rows returned twice).',
   ['right join', 'full outer join', 'union', 'emulate', 'outer join'],
   ["SELECT s.ID FROM shippers s LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.`Shipper ID` = s.ID UNION SELECT s.ID FROM shippers s RIGHT JOIN orders o ON o.`Shipper ID` = s.ID;"], 'left_join'),
 T('TH-45', 'Explicit JOIN syntax vs comma (implicit) join?',
   'FROM a JOIN b ON a.k = b.k is the explicit (ANSI) form: the join condition is separated from the filters, outer joins are possible, and forgetting ON is a syntax error. FROM a, b WHERE a.k = b.k is the old implicit form: same result for inner joins, but a forgotten condition silently creates a Cartesian product. Prefer explicit JOIN.',
   ['join syntax', 'implicit join', 'explicit join', 'comma join', 'ansi join', 'where join'],
   ["SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders o, customers c WHERE c.ID = o.`Customer ID`;"], 'inner_join'),
 T('TH-46', 'Condition in ON vs WHERE for an outer join?',
   'For INNER JOIN they are equivalent. For LEFT JOIN a condition on the RIGHT table in ON keeps unmatched left rows (right columns become NULL); the same condition in WHERE removes those rows and effectively turns the query into an inner join. Put filters on the right table in ON when you must keep all left rows.',
   ['on vs where', 'left join where', 'outer join filter', 'join condition', 'left join becomes inner'],
   ["SELECT c.ID, COUNT(o.`Order ID`) FROM customers c LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.`Customer ID` = c.ID AND o.`Order Date` >= '2006-03-01' GROUP BY c.ID LIMIT 3;"], 'left_join',
   ['WHERE o.`Order Date` >= ... after a LEFT JOIN.']),
 T('TH-47', 'INTERSECT and EXCEPT in MySQL?',
   'INTERSECT returns rows present in both queries; EXCEPT returns rows of the first query missing from the second (both remove duplicates by default). MySQL supports them only from 8.0.31; on older servers (such as 8.0.28) use IN / EXISTS for INTERSECT and NOT EXISTS / LEFT JOIN ... IS NULL for EXCEPT.',
   ['intersect', 'except', 'minus', 'set operations', 'mysql version', 'common rows'],
   ["SELECT `Product ID` FROM `order details` WHERE `Product ID` IN (SELECT `Product ID` FROM `purchase order details`) LIMIT 3;"], 'set_ops'),
 T('TH-48', 'ANY vs ALL vs SOME?',
   '> ANY (subquery) is true if the value is greater than AT LEAST ONE returned value (= greater than the minimum). > ALL is true if greater than EVERY returned value (= greater than the maximum). SOME is a synonym of ANY. = ANY is the same as IN; <> ALL is the same as NOT IN. ALL over an empty set is true.',
   ['any', 'all', 'some', 'quantified comparison', 'subquery', 'greater than all'],
   ["SELECT COUNT(*) FROM products WHERE `List Price` > ALL (SELECT `List Price` FROM products WHERE Category = 'Beverages');"], 'all_any'),
 T('TH-49', 'Scalar, row, table (derived) and correlated subqueries?',
   'Scalar: returns one value (usable in SELECT/WHERE). Row: one row, several columns, compared with (a, b) = (subquery). Table/derived: returns a result set used in FROM and must have an alias. Correlated: references the outer query and is evaluated per outer row (logically).',
   ['subquery types', 'scalar subquery', 'row subquery', 'derived table', 'inline view', 'correlated subquery'],
   ["SELECT `Product Name` FROM products WHERE `List Price` = (SELECT MAX(`List Price`) FROM products);"], 'subquery'),
 T('TH-50', 'What is a recursive CTE?',
   'WITH RECURSIVE name AS (anchor query UNION ALL recursive query referencing name) SELECT ... repeats the recursive part until it returns no rows (MySQL 8.0+). Used for hierarchies (employee-manager), number series and paths. Add a stop condition or LIMIT to avoid infinite loops.',
   ['recursive cte', 'with recursive', 'hierarchy', 'tree', 'series', 'cte'],
   ["WITH RECURSIVE n AS (SELECT 1 AS i UNION ALL SELECT i + 1 FROM n WHERE i < 5) SELECT i FROM n;"], 'subquery'),
 T('TH-51', 'What are window functions and how do they differ from GROUP BY?',
   'A window function computes over a set of related rows (OVER (PARTITION BY ... ORDER BY ...)) but keeps every row, while GROUP BY collapses rows. Examples: ROW_NUMBER/RANK/DENSE_RANK, LAG/LEAD, running totals SUM(x) OVER (ORDER BY d). MySQL 8.0+. They cannot be used directly in WHERE; wrap the query in a derived table.',
   ['window function', 'over', 'partition by', 'running total', 'lag', 'lead', 'analytic function', 'difference group by'],
   ["SELECT `Order ID`, `Shipping Fee`, SUM(`Shipping Fee`) OVER (ORDER BY `Order ID`) AS running_total FROM orders LIMIT 5;"], 'topn_rank'),
 T('TH-52', 'How do you get the top N rows per group?',
   'Number the rows inside each group and keep the first N: SELECT * FROM (SELECT t.*, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY group_col ORDER BY sort_col DESC) rn FROM t) x WHERE rn <= N. Use RANK/DENSE_RANK if ties must be kept. Without window functions use a correlated subquery that counts better rows.',
   ['top n per group', 'row_number', 'partition by', 'greatest n per group', 'rank per category'],
   ["SELECT Category, `Product Name`, `List Price` FROM (SELECT p.*, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY Category ORDER BY `List Price` DESC, ID) rn FROM products p) x WHERE rn <= 2;"], 'topn_rank'),
 T('TH-53', 'How do you find the Nth highest value?',
   'Distinct-value rank: SELECT DISTINCT col FROM t ORDER BY col DESC LIMIT 1 OFFSET N-1, or DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY col DESC) = N (keeps ties). Row position: ORDER BY col DESC LIMIT 1 OFFSET N-1 without DISTINCT counts duplicates as separate ranks. State which meaning the question needs.',
   ['nth highest', 'second highest', 'third highest', 'dense_rank', 'offset', 'distinct values', 'ranking'],
   ["SELECT DISTINCT `Shipping Fee` FROM orders ORDER BY `Shipping Fee` DESC LIMIT 1 OFFSET 1;"], 'topn_rank'),
 T('TH-54', 'How do you find duplicate rows?',
   'Group by the columns that should be unique and keep groups with more than one row: SELECT col, COUNT(*) FROM t GROUP BY col HAVING COUNT(*) > 1. To list the duplicate rows themselves, join back or use a self join with a.id < b.id.',
   ['duplicates', 'find duplicate rows', 'having count', 'duplicate values', 'data quality'],
   ["SELECT `Product Code`, COUNT(*) FROM products GROUP BY `Product Code` HAVING COUNT(*) > 1;"], 'group_having'),
 T('TH-55', 'What is GROUP_CONCAT?',
   'A MySQL aggregate that joins the values of a group into one string: GROUP_CONCAT(DISTINCT col ORDER BY col SEPARATOR \', \'). The result is cut at group_concat_max_len (default 1024 bytes).',
   ['group_concat', 'string aggregation', 'concatenate rows', 'list values per group'],
   ["SELECT Category, GROUP_CONCAT(`Product Name` ORDER BY `Product Name` SEPARATOR '; ') FROM products GROUP BY Category LIMIT 2;"], 'aggregate'),
 T('TH-56', 'What is ONLY_FULL_GROUP_BY?',
   'A MySQL SQL mode (default since 5.7) that rejects a GROUP BY query whose SELECT list contains a column that is neither grouped nor aggregated nor functionally dependent on the grouped columns. Fix it by adding the column to GROUP BY, aggregating it (MAX, ANY_VALUE) or restructuring with a subquery.',
   ['only_full_group_by', 'group by error', 'nonaggregated column', 'sql mode', 'error 1055'],
   ["SELECT @@sql_mode LIKE '%ONLY_FULL_GROUP_BY%';"], 'group_having'),
 T('TH-57', 'Can you use HAVING without GROUP BY?',
   'Yes: the whole result is one group, so HAVING filters that single aggregate row, e.g. SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders HAVING COUNT(*) > 10 returns a row or nothing. In practice HAVING is used after GROUP BY; row conditions belong in WHERE.',
   ['having without group by', 'having', 'aggregate filter', 'single group'],
   ["SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders HAVING COUNT(*) > 10;"], 'group_having'),
 T('TH-58', 'How does ORDER BY treat NULL?',
   'MySQL treats NULL as the lowest value: NULLs come first in ASC and last in DESC. To control it, sort on an expression first: ORDER BY col IS NULL, col (NULLs last) or ORDER BY col IS NOT NULL, col (NULLs first). MySQL has no NULLS FIRST/LAST keyword.',
   ['order by null', 'nulls first', 'nulls last', 'sorting nulls', 'sort order'],
   ["SELECT `Order ID`, `Shipped Date` FROM orders ORDER BY `Shipped Date` IS NULL, `Shipped Date` LIMIT 3;"], 'null_handling'),
 T('TH-59', 'COUNT(*) vs COUNT(1)?',
   'They are equivalent: both count every row, including rows with NULLs, and MySQL optimises them identically. COUNT(column) differs because it skips NULLs; COUNT(DISTINCT column) counts different non-NULL values.',
   ['count(1)', 'count(*)', 'count star', 'difference', 'count one'],
   ["SELECT COUNT(*), COUNT(1), COUNT(`Shipped Date`) FROM orders;"], 'aggregate'),
 T('TH-60', 'What does the / operator return in MySQL, and what is DIV?',
   '/ always returns a decimal result (5 / 2 = 2.5000); DIV performs integer division (5 DIV 2 = 2); % or MOD gives the remainder. Division by zero returns NULL (and a warning) unless strict error mode applies to the statement. Use NULLIF(divisor, 0) to be explicit.',
   ['division', 'integer division', 'div', 'modulo', 'divide by zero', 'operator'],
   ["SELECT 5 / 2, 5 DIV 2, 5 % 2, 5 / NULLIF(0, 0);"], 'computed'),
 T('TH-61', 'ROUND vs CEIL vs FLOOR vs TRUNCATE?',
   'ROUND(x, d) rounds to d decimals (half away from zero for exact values). CEIL(x) rounds up to an integer, FLOOR(x) rounds down, TRUNCATE(x, d) cuts the decimals without rounding. For negative numbers CEIL(-1.5) = -1 and FLOOR(-1.5) = -2.',
   ['round', 'ceil', 'ceiling', 'floor', 'truncate', 'rounding functions', 'decimals'],
   ["SELECT ROUND(2.567, 2), CEIL(2.1), FLOOR(2.9), TRUNCATE(2.567, 2);"], 'computed'),
 T('TH-62', 'DATEDIFF vs TIMESTAMPDIFF?',
   'DATEDIFF(end, start) returns the number of days between two dates (time part ignored; negative if the first is earlier). TIMESTAMPDIFF(unit, start, end) returns whole units (SECOND, MINUTE, HOUR, DAY, MONTH, YEAR) and takes the earlier date FIRST. Mixing the argument order flips the sign.',
   ['datediff', 'timestampdiff', 'date difference', 'days between', 'date functions'],
   ["SELECT DATEDIFF('2006-03-10', '2006-03-01'), TIMESTAMPDIFF(DAY, '2006-03-01', '2006-03-10');"], 'date'),
 T('TH-63', 'DATE_FORMAT format specifiers?',
   '%Y four-digit year, %y two-digit year, %m month 01-12, %c month 1-12, %M month name, %d day 01-31, %e day 1-31, %H hour 00-23, %i minutes, %s seconds, %W weekday name. Common mistake: %m is month but %i is minutes; DD/MM/YYYY is %d/%m/%Y.',
   ['date_format', 'format date', 'yyyy-mm', 'month name', 'date format specifiers', 'str_to_date'],
   ["SELECT DATE_FORMAT('2006-03-05 14:30:00', '%Y-%m'), DATE_FORMAT('2006-03-05', '%d/%m/%Y'), DATE_FORMAT('2006-03-05', '%M %e');"], 'date'),
 T('TH-64', 'LENGTH vs CHAR_LENGTH?',
   'LENGTH returns the number of BYTES, CHAR_LENGTH the number of CHARACTERS. They differ for multi-byte characters (accented letters). Use CHAR_LENGTH for text length.',
   ['length', 'char_length', 'string length', 'bytes vs characters', 'string functions'],
   ["SELECT LENGTH('abc'), CHAR_LENGTH('abc');"], 'string_funcs'),
 T('TH-65', 'What are INNER-join-based UPDATE and DELETE? (multi-table)',
   'MySQL allows joining in UPDATE and DELETE: UPDATE a JOIN b ON ... SET a.col = b.col WHERE ...; DELETE a FROM a JOIN b ON ... WHERE .... Always test the same join with a SELECT first and run the modification inside a transaction so it can be rolled back.',
   ['update join', 'delete join', 'multi-table update', 'modify with join', 'safe update'],
   ["START TRANSACTION; UPDATE orders o JOIN customers c ON c.ID = o.`Customer ID` SET o.Notes = o.Notes WHERE c.ID = 1; ROLLBACK;"]),
 T('TH-66', 'INSERT ... ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE vs REPLACE?',
   'INSERT ... ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE inserts a row or, when a primary/unique key already exists, updates the listed columns in place. REPLACE deletes the old row and inserts a new one (other columns reset to defaults, delete triggers/foreign keys can fire). INSERT IGNORE skips duplicates silently.',
   ['on duplicate key update', 'replace', 'upsert', 'insert ignore', 'duplicate key'],
   ["CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE tmp_u (id INT PRIMARY KEY, n INT); INSERT INTO tmp_u VALUES (1, 1) ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE n = n + 1; INSERT INTO tmp_u VALUES (1, 1) ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE n = n + 1; SELECT * FROM tmp_u;"]),
 T('TH-67', 'What are constraints (NOT NULL, UNIQUE, CHECK, DEFAULT)?',
   'NOT NULL forbids NULL; UNIQUE forbids duplicates; PRIMARY KEY = NOT NULL + UNIQUE; FOREIGN KEY enforces references; CHECK (condition) restricts values (enforced from MySQL 8.0.16, ignored before); DEFAULT supplies a value when none is given. They keep data valid at the database level.',
   ['constraints', 'not null', 'unique', 'check constraint', 'default', 'data integrity'],
   ["CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE tmp_k (id INT PRIMARY KEY, qty INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0 CHECK (qty >= 0)); INSERT INTO tmp_k (id) VALUES (1); SELECT * FROM tmp_k;"]),
 T('TH-68', 'What is AUTO_INCREMENT and a surrogate key?',
   'AUTO_INCREMENT generates the next integer for a column automatically (usually the primary key); LAST_INSERT_ID() returns the last generated value of the session. A surrogate key is such an artificial id with no business meaning, as opposed to a natural key (e.g. email, product code) taken from the data. Gaps can occur after rollbacks or deletes.',
   ['auto_increment', 'surrogate key', 'natural key', 'identity', 'last_insert_id', 'serial'],
   ["CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE tmp_a (id INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY, v INT); INSERT INTO tmp_a (v) VALUES (10), (20); SELECT * FROM tmp_a;"]),
 T('TH-69', 'Stored procedure vs function vs trigger?',
   'A stored procedure is called with CALL, can take IN/OUT parameters and run many statements. A stored function returns one value and can be used inside a SELECT. A trigger runs automatically BEFORE/AFTER an INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE on a table (for auditing, validation). All three keep logic on the server.',
   ['stored procedure', 'function', 'trigger', 'routine', 'difference', 'programmability'], []),
 T('TH-70', 'What are views used for, and are they updatable?',
   'A view is a saved SELECT that behaves like a table: it simplifies complex queries, hides columns and gives a stable interface. It stores no data (unless materialised, which MySQL lacks). Simple views (single table, no aggregation/DISTINCT/GROUP BY/UNION) are updatable; WITH CHECK OPTION rejects changes that would make a row disappear from the view.',
   ['view', 'updatable view', 'create view', 'with check option', 'virtual table', 'security'],
   ["CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE tmp_v AS SELECT ID, Company FROM customers; SELECT COUNT(*) FROM tmp_v;"], 'subquery'),
 T('TH-71', 'How do you prevent SQL injection?',
   'Never build SQL by concatenating user input. Use prepared statements / parameterised queries so the input is sent as data, not as SQL; validate input; give the application account only the privileges it needs. Escaping alone is fragile.',
   ['sql injection', 'prepared statement', 'parameterized query', 'security', 'sanitize input'],
   ["PREPARE s FROM 'SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders WHERE `Customer ID` = ?'; SET @c = 3; EXECUTE s USING @c; DEALLOCATE PREPARE s;"]),
 T('TH-72', 'Collation and case sensitivity?',
   'A collation defines how strings compare and sort. Default MySQL 8 collations such as utf8mb4_0900_ai_ci are case-insensitive and accent-insensitive (ci, ai), so \'abc\' = \'ABC\'. Use a binary/_cs collation or BINARY for case-sensitive comparison.',
   ['collation', 'case sensitive', 'case insensitive', 'utf8mb4', 'string comparison', 'character set'],
   ["SELECT 'abc' = 'ABC', BINARY 'abc' = 'ABC';"]),
 T('TH-73', 'LIKE vs REGEXP?',
   'LIKE supports only % (any string) and _ (one character). REGEXP / REGEXP_LIKE supports full regular expressions (character classes, alternation, anchors) but cannot use an index and is slower. Prefer LIKE \'abc%\' when a simple prefix match is enough.',
   ['like', 'regexp', 'regular expression', 'pattern matching', 'rlike', 'wildcard'],
   ["SELECT COUNT(*) FROM customers WHERE `Job Title` REGEXP 'Manager|Assistant';"], 'select_filter'),
 T('TH-74', 'IN vs OR vs BETWEEN?',
   'IN (a, b, c) is shorthand for several OR equalities on one column. BETWEEN x AND y is an inclusive range (>= x AND <= y) and is risky for datetimes. IN with a NULL in the list never matches NULL; NOT IN with a NULL in the list returns nothing.',
   ['in', 'or', 'between', 'range', 'list membership', 'difference'],
   ["SELECT COUNT(*) FROM products WHERE Category IN ('Pasta', 'Soups', 'Beverages');"], 'in_notin'),
 T('TH-75', 'What does an aggregate return on an empty set?',
   'COUNT returns 0; SUM, AVG, MIN and MAX return NULL. Wrap with COALESCE(SUM(x), 0) when a zero is wanted. With GROUP BY an empty input produces no groups, hence no rows.',
   ['empty set', 'no rows', 'aggregate empty', 'sum null', 'count zero'],
   ["SELECT COUNT(*), SUM(`Shipping Fee`), COALESCE(SUM(`Shipping Fee`), 0) FROM orders WHERE `Order ID` < 0;"], 'null_handling'),
 T('TH-76', 'EXISTS vs COUNT(*) > 0, and why SELECT 1 inside EXISTS?',
   'EXISTS stops at the first matching row, COUNT(*) > 0 must count all matches, so EXISTS is usually cheaper. The select list inside EXISTS is ignored, so SELECT 1 (or *) is only a convention.',
   ['exists', 'count', 'existence check', 'select 1', 'performance'],
   ["SELECT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM orders WHERE `Customer ID` = 3);"], 'exists'),
 T('TH-77', 'Temporary table vs CTE vs derived table?',
   'A derived table is an inline subquery in FROM (alias required, one statement). A CTE (WITH) is a named result for one statement, reusable several times in it and more readable. A temporary table is a real table visible only in the session until dropped: use it to reuse an intermediate result across several statements or to index it.',
   ['temporary table', 'cte', 'derived table', 'with clause', 'difference', 'intermediate result'],
   ["CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE tmp_t AS SELECT `Customer ID`, COUNT(*) n FROM orders GROUP BY `Customer ID`; SELECT COUNT(*) FROM tmp_t;"], 'subquery'),
 T('TH-78', 'What is the difference between a primary key and a clustered index, and what is a natural vs surrogate key choice?',
   'The primary key is a logical constraint (unique, not null); in InnoDB it is also the clustered index that physically orders the rows. Short, stable, increasing keys (AUTO_INCREMENT integers) keep the clustered index compact and avoid page splits; long random keys (UUID strings) make every secondary index bigger.',
   ['primary key', 'clustered index', 'key choice', 'uuid', 'innodb', 'natural key'], [], 'optimization'),
 T('TH-79', 'Which is faster: JOIN or subquery, and what makes a query slow?',
   'There is no universal winner: MySQL 8 rewrites many IN/EXISTS subqueries into semi-joins. Slowness usually comes from missing indexes (full scans), functions on indexed columns, SELECT *, joining before filtering large tables, correlated subqueries on big tables, and large OFFSETs. Measure with EXPLAIN rather than assuming.',
   ['join vs subquery', 'slow query', 'query performance', 'optimizer', 'semi join', 'tuning', 'why slow'],
   ["EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM customers WHERE ID IN (SELECT `Customer ID` FROM orders);"], 'optimization'),
 T('TH-80', 'What are relational algebra operations?',
   'Selection (sigma) = WHERE: choose rows. Projection (pi) = the SELECT column list: choose columns. Join combines rows of two relations on a condition. Union, intersection and difference are set operations (UNION, INTERSECT, EXCEPT). Rename (rho) = aliases (AS). Division answers "for all" questions (relational division).',
   ['relational algebra', 'selection', 'projection', 'join', 'set operations', 'division', 'theory'], []),
 T('TH-81', 'What is a database schema, and what is the difference between a database and a DBMS?',
   'A schema is the structure of a database: tables, columns, data types, keys and relationships. A database is the stored, organised data; the DBMS (MySQL, PostgreSQL ...) is the software that stores, queries and protects it. In MySQL the words "schema" and "database" are synonyms.',
   ['schema', 'database', 'dbms', 'rdbms', 'definition', 'basics'],
   ["SELECT DATABASE();"]),
 T('TH-82', 'What is cardinality of a relationship (1:1, 1:N, N:M)?',
   'One-to-one: each row relates to at most one row on the other side. One-to-many: one parent row relates to many child rows (customers -> orders): the foreign key sits on the many side. Many-to-many needs a junction table holding both keys (products <-> orders through `order details`).',
   ['cardinality', 'one to many', 'many to many', 'junction table', 'relationship types', 'er diagram', 'bridge table'],
   ["SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT `Order ID`), COUNT(DISTINCT `Product ID`), COUNT(*) FROM `order details`;"], 'inner_join'),
]
